 ## Conclusion

-Across six linked audits, Hydrosheaf reduced groundwater interpretive
-non-uniqueness only conditionally. Chemistry was robustly complementary;
-age and hydraulics changed uncertainty, and hydraulics improved calibration,
-without a multiplicity-corrected topology-ranking gain. Correct topology
-improved age inference, reversed topology degraded or destabilised it,
-carbonate processes remained unrecovered, and every adverse control showed
-that lower uncertainty can coexist with worse discrimination or calibration.
-The Northern Ghana data support component diagnostics and explicit
-non-identifiability mapping, not field validation of exact topology, age, or
-unique reactions.
-
-The added system and representation audits answer the central software-
-science question directly. The released public pipeline executed
-reproducibly but did not establish an overall full-sheaf advantage. In M7.4,
-the affine sheaf collapsed exactly to the graph solution in the identity
-limit, outperformed an identity graph and a restriction-map permutation
-control, and localised planted cycle conflicts, but did not outperform the
-strong edge-local graph overall and had worse log loss. The independent M7.5
-follow-up showed why that result should not be simplified: restoring direct
-local evidence improved the original global estimator, whereas stronger LOO
-robustification worsened calibration. A local-first/global-fallback hybrid
-improved overall PR-AUC and produced reproducible gains for incompatible
-cycles and noisy/missing observations, but did not satisfy the full Brier and
-log-loss gate. The sheaf layer has therefore earned a bounded scientific-
-workflow role as an explicit model of non-identity relations, global
-compatibility and selective incomplete-endpoint support. The LOO robust estimator
-has not earned excellence status, and the sheaf has not earned a general claim
-of predictive superiority over weighted graphs. That narrower conclusion is
-the one supported by the locked results and should govern the thesis, viva and
-future field validation.
-
